WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.856
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:10.650
MATT LEONARD: Good
morning, everybody.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:13.150
Thanks for joining us
here at Google Next.

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:14.150
My name is Matt Leonard.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:17.672
I'm a product manager in the
Google Cloud billing platform.

00:00:17.672 --> 00:00:20.450
Before joining Google Cloud and
joining the billing team here,

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:24.370
I actually worked in
billing at Azure and AWS,

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:27.770
so someone who you'd call
a self-inflicted billing

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:30.855
Cloud expert, unfortunately.

00:00:30.855 --> 00:00:33.270
Today, I want to talk
about financial governance

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:34.895
for managing your
Cloud business.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.660
So I think most of us
here in the IT industry

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:46.190
can agree that Cloud has
disrupted the way that we

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:49.250
manage our business and how we
go about procuring and managing

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:50.335
infrastructure.

00:00:53.104 --> 00:00:54.270
We're focusing on expanding.

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.436
It's changed the way that
we buy our infrastructure.

00:00:56.436 --> 00:01:00.525
In a traditional model, it's
a very centralized processes.

00:01:00.525 --> 00:01:01.470
It's controlled.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:02.850
It's predictable.

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:04.769
Everything is run
through a budget cycle.

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:07.020
We have financial controls,
checks and balances,

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:10.260
to ensure that spending
doesn't get out of control.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:12.540
The Cloud's disrupted that.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:14.700
Anyone in your
company with access

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:18.060
can now spin up instances that
you don't have any visibility

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.874
to, and you end up
reacting to cost,

00:01:19.874 --> 00:01:21.040
rather than planning for it.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:27.115
This IT expanding, it's moved
from a CapEx expense, which

00:01:27.115 --> 00:01:28.864
is predictable over a
long period of time,

00:01:28.864 --> 00:01:31.640
to an OpEx expense, which is
coming out on a monthly basis,

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:36.660
and you're managing and
trying to control that spend.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.560
So to play on this
metaphor, you wouldn't

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:39.976
build a ship that's
full of holes,

00:01:39.976 --> 00:01:41.890
and you don't want to
approach your Cloud

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:43.330
billing in the same way.

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:44.140
You need a plan.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:47.722
You need a strategy for
your financial governance.

00:01:47.722 --> 00:01:49.430
Lack of financial
governance in the Cloud

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:50.971
can be kind of
shocking if it happens

00:01:50.971 --> 00:01:52.550
to you as an organization.

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:56.326
I have a couple of examples that
I wanted to share of incidents

00:01:56.326 --> 00:01:58.450
that I've been involved
with in my years of working

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:00.000
in cloud billing.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.940
We had a customer recently
that was doing a load and scale

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:05.200
test.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.759
They spun up 9,000 instances,
ran their load test.

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:12.460
A few days later, they realized
they hadn't switched up

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:13.810
the environment.

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:15.250
Whoops.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:17.555
It was an expensive
problem have.

00:02:17.555 --> 00:02:19.330
I also worked with
an ISP at one point.

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:21.880
They were doing a
solution that they

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:23.530
wanted to run on multi-cloud.

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:25.510
So they used
clarification on AWS

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:27.250
and they spun up
their templates.

00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:28.750
And the way the
CloudFormation works

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:32.360
is when you do a deployment
and that deployment fails,

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:35.120
CloudFormation is stateful, and
it rolls back the resources.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:36.340
They then went over
to Azure, and they

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:37.225
started using our templates.

00:02:37.225 --> 00:02:39.470
And they're launching
instances, and off they go.

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:40.990
Well, ARM is not stateful.

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:44.020
It doesn't rollback instances
in a failed deployment.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:47.470
This customer may end up with
a $350,000 bill for Azure

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:49.900
because they weren't
watching their spend.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.090
But this doesn't
have to be a reality.

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:55.270
Your reality can be much more
controlled and predictable.

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:58.090
We had another customer
recently that, basically--

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:00.920
application, engineer
checked in some code.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:02.920
Their normal run rate
for the storage expenses

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:04.900
on this application
was about 0.04%

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:07.420
of their application's
cost profile.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.700
It went to 45% over night.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:11.560
Luckily, they were
monitoring this.

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:13.082
They had controls in place.

00:03:13.082 --> 00:03:15.040
They saw the change in
the application profile,

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:20.534
were able to rollback that
code, and control their expense.

00:03:20.534 --> 00:03:22.450
So that's what we're
here today to talk about,

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:24.590
what tools do we have in
the Google Cloud toolkit

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:26.020
that are going to help you
with your financial-governance

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:26.990
strategy.

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:28.385
And then we have Lucas
here from the Broad who

00:03:28.385 --> 00:03:30.140
is going to talk about
how they managed it

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:31.223
at scale for the business.

00:03:37.877 --> 00:03:40.210
So some of the quick questions
you need to ask yourself,

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:42.543
when you think about your
financial-governance strategy,

00:03:42.543 --> 00:03:44.861
is how is my cost organized?

00:03:44.861 --> 00:03:46.360
If you attended the
session earlier,

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:48.110
James Byers did a
session called--

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:50.610
and sorry, I'm just going to
make sure I get it right here--

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:53.200
Managing Costs in GCP, How
to Structure Your Resources.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.210
And this laid out some
of the best practices.

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:57.010
And so if you weren't
able to attend that,

00:03:57.010 --> 00:03:58.210
I'd suggest that
you catch it online.

00:03:58.210 --> 00:04:00.340
But it talks about some of
the different mechanisms

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:03.050
that you have in Google
Cloud to organize your cost.

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:05.020
So we have folders.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:07.624
We have projects,
and we have labels.

00:04:07.624 --> 00:04:09.040
And those are
different mechanisms

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.581
by which you can
organize your costs,

00:04:10.581 --> 00:04:13.720
either in a structured
hierarchy or in a crosscutting

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:14.832
or cross-project way.

00:04:14.832 --> 00:04:16.540
But it's pretty
important, because that's

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.831
what you're going to bind
all your financial-governance

00:04:18.831 --> 00:04:20.306
policy to, how you
organize costs.

00:04:20.306 --> 00:04:22.180
And you're probably not
going to want to look

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:23.359
at it at a single level.

00:04:23.359 --> 00:04:25.900
You're going to want to look at
it by project, which probably

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:29.530
represents a team, or an
application, or an environment.

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:31.780
But you might want to also
look at it by organization.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:32.614
I have a department.

00:04:32.614 --> 00:04:34.321
I want to see how much
this department is

00:04:34.321 --> 00:04:35.230
costing at totality.

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:38.720
So I want to monitor
at multiple levels.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.660
Second, what is my budget?

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:42.490
This is really important.

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:45.340
How much do I actually
expect to spend?

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:47.680
So we're going to look at,
like, where have I been?

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:49.557
How much did it
cost me in the past?

00:04:49.557 --> 00:04:50.890
Where is it going in the future?

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:53.410
How is my cost projected
at the current run rate?

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:54.940
And then what if--

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:56.740
what if we changed
the characteristics

00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:59.073
of this application, changed
the profile of the machines

00:04:59.073 --> 00:05:00.010
that we're using?

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:02.350
What if we had a 4x increase
in the amount of traffic

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:04.120
that's going to
hit this platform?

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:05.550
We want to play out
some of these scenarios

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:07.383
so that we can understand
what is my budget,

00:05:07.383 --> 00:05:09.830
and how am I going
to manage that?

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:11.700
And then lastly, what
is the budget impact?

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:13.158
This is kind of an
interesting one.

00:05:13.158 --> 00:05:15.670
What would you want to do
when you get to your budget?

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:17.369
The most passive
form of impact is

00:05:17.369 --> 00:05:18.910
I'm going to just
notify some people.

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:20.440
I'm going to let you know
that something is happening

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:22.060
that you weren't expecting.

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:24.160
But it can be much,
much more intrusive.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:26.320
I hear customers a lot
who talk about capping.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:27.180
I want to cap.

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:29.320
Actually, I'm fine if you
delete all my resources,

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:32.620
you blow away all my storage,
and you make this never exist,

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:34.370
because I don't want
to pay another penny.

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:36.411
And Lucas will talk about
some of those use cases

00:05:36.411 --> 00:05:38.260
himself, actually,
because it's something

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:39.885
that you hear in
dev-test environments,

00:05:39.885 --> 00:05:42.830
in students, where they actually
can't pay another penny.

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:45.074
And so they need
to stop that cost.

00:05:45.074 --> 00:05:46.990
More than likely, you
probably have a strategy

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:47.865
somewhere in between.

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:56.930
So-- oops.

00:05:56.930 --> 00:05:57.740
Did I skip a slide?

00:05:57.740 --> 00:05:59.042
No, I did not.

00:05:59.042 --> 00:06:00.500
So our goal here
at Google Cloud is

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:03.659
to give you a toolkit, because
there is no one size fits all.

00:06:03.659 --> 00:06:05.450
What you run in your
production environment

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:06.710
may be very, very
different to what

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:08.210
you want to put in a
sandbox environment

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:10.126
so that people can play
around with the Cloud.

00:06:10.126 --> 00:06:13.665
So we want to give you
lots of different options.

00:06:13.665 --> 00:06:16.040
So today, we're going to talk
about a few different tools

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:16.520
in the toolkit.

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:18.040
We're going to talk
about promotions.

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:18.770
We are going to
talk about quota,

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:20.150
and we're going to talk
about budget, and how you

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:21.900
can use those in your business.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:24.400
And then Lucas is going to give
you some real-life examples.

00:06:28.007 --> 00:06:28.840
This is permissions.

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:30.350
I'm just going explain this
diagram for a little bit,

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:32.030
because I'm being told that
it's a little bit distracting

00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:33.280
with the way it keeps spinning.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:35.140
Essentially, you want to operate
in an environment of least

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:35.872
privilege.

00:06:35.872 --> 00:06:37.330
So you can see here
in this diagram

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:40.780
that, effectively, a user is
allowed to access resources

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:44.070
and launch them, unless
permission denies it.

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:45.747
The way that you
use least privilege

00:06:45.747 --> 00:06:47.830
is going to differ, again,
between your production

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:49.650
environment and the
sandbox environment.

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:51.108
In your production
environment, you

00:06:51.108 --> 00:06:53.809
might have a very
strict permissions

00:06:53.809 --> 00:06:56.350
model, where only a few users
can access and launch resources

00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:57.820
in that production environment.

00:06:57.820 --> 00:06:59.361
But you may have
somewhat of a looser

00:06:59.361 --> 00:07:02.414
financial-governance
model, where you don't

00:07:02.414 --> 00:07:03.580
want to terminate resources.

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:04.892
You don't want to cap spending.

00:07:04.892 --> 00:07:06.100
You just want to be notified.

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:07.490
It's a lot more passive.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:08.990
Whereas in your
sandbox environment,

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:11.890
you might want a very strict
financial-governance policy

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:14.470
that says, I want to
terminate these resources when

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:17.290
my cost gets to foo,
or you can only launch

00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:19.321
so many different instances.

00:07:19.321 --> 00:07:21.320
But you may have a very
loose permissions model.

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:25.240
Anybody can spend up one of
these non-production accounts,

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:26.740
as long as it's
financially gallant.

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:32.180
Quota.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:35.780
Quota basically enables you to
build a virtual data center.

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:38.360
It basically sets the
maximum concurrent resources

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:39.320
that you can operate.

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:42.620
Now, today, the way that Quota
operates is essentially by SKU.

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:44.870
And so an example of that
is the number of cores

00:07:44.870 --> 00:07:46.280
that you can launch for compute.

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:49.502
So if you set a Quota control
that says, I have 100 cores.

00:07:49.502 --> 00:07:51.710
That's like a physical data
center where you can only

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:54.680
put so many VMs into rack, and
you can only put so many racks

00:07:54.680 --> 00:07:56.240
into a physical-data center.

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:58.670
And so you've actually
controlled the maximum amount

00:07:58.670 --> 00:08:01.400
of resources that a
user can spend up,

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:03.480
and so therefore, you've
controlled the cost.

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:05.990
It's not going to be
more than 100 bitcoin,

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:08.690
or whatever 100
VMs will cost you,

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:11.120
because you've maximized the
amount that they can spend.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:13.130
Now, they might not
spend 100 cores,

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:15.030
but they can spend
up to 100 cores.

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:16.970
Now, if it's a
dev-test environment,

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:19.410
and somebody is just playing
around with an environment,

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:22.346
you might want to set that
to 3, for example, which

00:08:22.346 --> 00:08:23.720
is a very low
number and you know

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:26.690
that you're not going to
have a large cost spend-out.

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:29.501
So this is what a Quota
environment looks like right

00:08:29.501 --> 00:08:30.000
now.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:30.980
There's lots of
interesting things

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:32.605
that will be coming
down the track here

00:08:32.605 --> 00:08:37.289
that we can talk about in a Q&amp;A
session, if you're interested.

00:08:37.289 --> 00:08:39.380
But basically, that
Quota enables you

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:41.434
to throttle your total spend.

00:08:41.434 --> 00:08:44.059
But you have to think about what
that does to your environment.

00:08:44.059 --> 00:08:45.434
If it's a production
environment,

00:08:45.434 --> 00:08:47.900
is it OK for you to have that
degradation of performance.

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:51.506
If you hit the 100 cores,
things are going to slow down.

00:08:51.506 --> 00:08:52.880
Or if you're in
BigQuery, and you

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:56.270
have a maximum amount of
slots, Query is going to sit.

00:08:56.270 --> 00:08:58.970
And they're going wait, waiting
for a slot to open up to run.

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:01.640
And so quotas great but
you have to think about how

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:04.560
that impacts your business.

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:06.530
Last weekend, we
talked about budgets.

00:09:06.530 --> 00:09:09.314
Budgets enable you to normalize
usage across everything

00:09:09.314 --> 00:09:10.230
that we have in Cloud.

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:14.950
Today, we have a lot of
SKUs, close to 100,000 SKUs--

00:09:14.950 --> 00:09:17.577
if you want to look at in
the myriad detail across all

00:09:17.577 --> 00:09:19.410
the different regions
and different products

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:21.540
and different ways that
you can spend things up.

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:23.610
And what budget allows
you to do is normalize it

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:25.284
into cost over time.

00:09:25.284 --> 00:09:26.700
So it becomes very
simple, and you

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:29.790
can decide what you want to do
as you hit your budget limits.

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:35.610
Today in Google Cloud, we let
you monitor a monthly budget

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:37.350
per project or per
billing account.

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:39.889
So you can specify that.

00:09:39.889 --> 00:09:41.430
And you can get a
simple notification

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:42.810
when you hit a preset threshold.

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:45.240
So you could say, I have
a 100 bitcoin to spend.

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:47.790
When I get to 50 bitcoin,
I want you to tell me,

00:09:47.790 --> 00:09:50.650
because I want to
see how I'm trending.

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:52.240
You can also automate
budget actions.

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:54.730
We recently launched
a new feature called

00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:56.470
programmatic notifications.

00:09:56.470 --> 00:09:59.041
This essentially allows you to
build your own budget policy.

00:09:59.041 --> 00:10:00.790
Now eventually, we
want to bring this over

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:02.179
to a function of the console.

00:10:02.179 --> 00:10:03.720
But today, what that
allows you to do

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:05.725
is to specify a
Cloud Function, like,

00:10:05.725 --> 00:10:06.850
a different set of actions.

00:10:06.850 --> 00:10:08.933
And we provide some basic
reference architectures,

00:10:08.933 --> 00:10:10.930
which will go into
in a bit more detail.

00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:13.900
But you can do things like
you can forward your budget

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:16.360
notification to different
mediums, like Slack.

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:17.740
Or you can suspend resources.

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:21.850
We gave an example policy of how
to take a budget notification

00:10:21.850 --> 00:10:23.790
and shut down all the
compute instances.

00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:24.790
Or you can cap spending.

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:26.710
You can actually
pull a project out

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:30.160
of a billing which will farm out
a termination of all resources

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:31.930
in that billing account.

00:10:31.930 --> 00:10:33.880
And so this is a
fairly powerful tool

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:35.740
with a fairly-simple
implementation.

00:10:35.740 --> 00:10:37.150
I had a customer
recently told me

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:39.150
it took them about seven
minutes to set it up,

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:42.860
so fairly straightforward.

00:10:42.860 --> 00:10:43.360
All right.

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:45.130
Without further ado, I'm
going to introduce Lucas

00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:46.300
from the Broad, and he is
going to talk about how

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:47.770
they operate at scale.

00:10:47.770 --> 00:10:50.260
LUKAS KARLSSON: Thank you, Matt.

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:50.900
Let's see here.

00:10:55.060 --> 00:10:56.870
OK.

00:10:56.870 --> 00:10:57.960
I'm Lukas Karlsson.

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:01.220
I work at the Broad Institute.

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:03.600
Am I going backwards here?

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:06.570
I've been with the Broad
for a little over 15 years.

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:10.440
I started exactly two weeks
after the Broad Institute

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:14.190
completed the Human
Genome Project in 2003.

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:16.530
And over the last
15 years, we've

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:21.570
sequenced something like 100,000
genomes, after that first one.

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:23.700
Started as a systems
administrator

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:27.570
and moved into a manager
of systems administrators.

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:30.330
And then I became a bit
of a software engineer,

00:11:30.330 --> 00:11:34.890
writing web applications
and other automation tools.

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:38.220
And at some point, we needed
to create a DevOps group,

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:41.340
so I took two years or so
and created a DevOps group

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:42.210
at the Broad.

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:44.610
And now, I'm responsible
for our Cloud environment

00:11:44.610 --> 00:11:46.800
and for the success
of our developers

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:49.704
in using that environment.

00:11:49.704 --> 00:11:51.120
If you don't know
about the Broad,

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:52.770
we are a research
institute doing

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:55.380
bioinformatics and
biology-type stuff

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:57.822
in Cambridge, Massachusetts.

00:11:57.822 --> 00:12:00.030
As I mentioned, we were one
of the major contributors

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:02.040
for the Human Genome Project.

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:03.922
We also have a bunch
of other things

00:12:03.922 --> 00:12:05.130
that you might have heard of.

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:08.430
For example, something
recent that we've worked on

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:12.060
is the CRISPR patent
for editing of genomes.

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:17.370
And we're organized around
major research areas

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:19.230
and major production services.

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:22.440
So for example, we have a
group that's focusing entirely

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:23.824
on cancer research.

00:12:23.824 --> 00:12:25.740
We have another group
that's focusing entirely

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:30.330
on psychiatric disease research,
like depression and bipolar.

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:33.000
And then the platforms
run like small businesses

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:35.770
within the Institute that
provide a production service.

00:12:35.770 --> 00:12:37.410
So for example, the
genomics platform

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:40.920
provides services around
extraction of DNA,

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:46.440
storing DNA, sequencing
DNA, and analyzing DNA.

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:49.840
We're funded from a number
of different sources.

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:52.092
So for example, we
have dozens and dozens

00:12:52.092 --> 00:12:54.300
of different government
grants for different projects

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:55.650
that we're working on.

00:12:55.650 --> 00:12:57.150
And each of those
grants, obviously,

00:12:57.150 --> 00:13:02.130
have certain constraints on
how much resources they grant.

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:05.070
We have lots of different
philanthropic gifts as well.

00:13:05.070 --> 00:13:07.950
And today, actually,
philanthropy has changed

00:13:07.950 --> 00:13:10.620
a little bit, so people
don't have a tendency as much

00:13:10.620 --> 00:13:12.750
to just give money to places.

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:15.250
They do this
venture philanthropy

00:13:15.250 --> 00:13:18.362
where they're investing in a
particular scientific outcome.

00:13:18.362 --> 00:13:20.070
So we have a lot of
those kinds of gifts.

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:22.290
And then we have some
inventions like CRISPR

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:24.660
and some software licensing
and stuff that we do,

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:27.414
that brings in some
revenue as well.

00:13:27.414 --> 00:13:29.080
Now, when we're talking
about the Cloud,

00:13:29.080 --> 00:13:31.290
there's a bunch of things
that we're concerned about.

00:13:31.290 --> 00:13:33.248
As I mentioned, because
so much of our research

00:13:33.248 --> 00:13:35.280
is funded by
government grants, we

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:37.760
have to be very thoughtful
about how we use those funds.

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:40.050
It's public funds first.

00:13:40.050 --> 00:13:42.660
And second, there is
a lot of constraints.

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:45.810
A given grant might only have
a certain amount of money

00:13:45.810 --> 00:13:48.540
available to it over
a certain time period.

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:51.450
And then only a
portion of that grant

00:13:51.450 --> 00:13:53.850
is what's going to be used
for the Cloud computing.

00:13:53.850 --> 00:13:55.800
A lot of it might be
used for the sequencing

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:57.615
or other parts of the process.

00:14:00.410 --> 00:14:03.110
And when we do overspend
on one of these grants,

00:14:03.110 --> 00:14:03.980
it's a real problem.

00:14:03.980 --> 00:14:07.130
Because if you have a grant
that has $10,000 of resources,

00:14:07.130 --> 00:14:09.680
and you mistakenly
spend $100,000,

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:12.336
well that grant is not going
to pay the extra $90,000

00:14:12.336 --> 00:14:12.960
that you spent.

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:14.944
We have to pull that
from some other place.

00:14:14.944 --> 00:14:16.610
And we can't pull it
from another grant,

00:14:16.610 --> 00:14:18.890
so we have to pull it from
our administrative funds

00:14:18.890 --> 00:14:21.955
that we use to run the business.

00:14:21.955 --> 00:14:23.580
Now, when we're using
the Public Cloud,

00:14:23.580 --> 00:14:26.530
there are some things that we're
particularly concerned about.

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:28.571
We have a lot of issues
that can come up that can

00:14:28.571 --> 00:14:30.220
affect the way you spend money.

00:14:30.220 --> 00:14:32.590
So for example, as
Matt alluded to,

00:14:32.590 --> 00:14:35.040
you can have developers who
are spending up instances,

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:36.540
and they just
leave them running.

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:37.860
And a month later,
you look at your bill,

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:40.443
and you're like, why did I spend
tens of thousands of dollars?

00:14:40.443 --> 00:14:43.180
And it's an oopsie situation.

00:14:43.180 --> 00:14:44.440
We also use a lot of storage.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:48.660
Storage is probably 60%
or 70% of our overall cost

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:50.230
of using the Cloud.

00:14:50.230 --> 00:14:53.100
So when we make a choice
to put a bunch of storage

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:57.090
into a global bucket, instead
of maybe a Nearline or Coldline

00:14:57.090 --> 00:14:59.310
bucket if that's the
way we intend to use it,

00:14:59.310 --> 00:15:00.876
that can drastically
change the cost.

00:15:00.876 --> 00:15:02.250
And so we want to
be able to keep

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:04.291
an eye on that kind of
stuff and really track it,

00:15:04.291 --> 00:15:07.090
so we make good decisions.

00:15:07.090 --> 00:15:08.730
We also periodically
have problems

00:15:08.730 --> 00:15:10.444
where people know
that they don't

00:15:10.444 --> 00:15:12.360
have enough resources
to do the work that they

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:15.810
want to do and do it anyway.

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:17.820
So they come, and they
ask for forgiveness

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:19.110
about this overspend issue.

00:15:19.110 --> 00:15:21.240
And we're still having to take
the money from somewhere else

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:22.281
in order to pay for that.

00:15:22.281 --> 00:15:24.850
So that's a real big issue.

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:27.612
Something that can
happen is periodically

00:15:27.612 --> 00:15:29.320
you will have a
developer make a mistake,

00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:32.920
like committing one of their
service accounts JSON keys

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:35.025
to a public GitHub repo.

00:15:35.025 --> 00:15:36.400
This is a great
experiment if you

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:39.430
want to see how quickly
cryptominers can find your key,

00:15:39.430 --> 00:15:43.000
get into your project, and
spend up lots of instances.

00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:44.890
And so many of you
who use the Cloud

00:15:44.890 --> 00:15:47.050
may have run into
situations like that.

00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:49.270
And so monitoring your
cost is very important,

00:15:49.270 --> 00:15:52.132
because that's even worse than
the developer, who spun up a VM

00:15:52.132 --> 00:15:54.340
and forgot to shut it down,
because they're literally

00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:56.710
going to use all of
the Quota that you have

00:15:56.710 --> 00:15:58.990
to try to make as much
crypto as possible

00:15:58.990 --> 00:16:01.810
over the smallest
period of time.

00:16:01.810 --> 00:16:03.740
And a lot of these
issues, when you're

00:16:03.740 --> 00:16:05.750
thinking about moving
a large institution

00:16:05.750 --> 00:16:08.420
from your on-prem
environment into the Cloud,

00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:11.510
they can create a real
obstacle to being successful.

00:16:11.510 --> 00:16:14.510
Because all these people who are
responsible for these grants,

00:16:14.510 --> 00:16:17.750
if they have a very controlled
and understood way of managing

00:16:17.750 --> 00:16:20.980
their resources in the
on-prem legacy environment,

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:23.120
and they don't have
that in the Cloud,

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:25.520
then that creates a real
fear and uncertainty

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:27.020
about moving to the Cloud.

00:16:27.020 --> 00:16:29.210
I know when I run jobs in
our on-prem environment

00:16:29.210 --> 00:16:31.220
exactly how much
it's going to cost.

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:34.640
If I go do it in the Cloud,
and I make some mistake,

00:16:34.640 --> 00:16:36.705
am I going to spend
all the Broad's money?

00:16:36.705 --> 00:16:37.580
That's a big concern.

00:16:37.580 --> 00:16:40.250
And that has a lot of people
second guessing the move

00:16:40.250 --> 00:16:40.890
to the Cloud.

00:16:40.890 --> 00:16:43.800
And so we want to solve that.

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:47.450
So most of the talks that
I give as Cloud architect

00:16:47.450 --> 00:16:50.350
at the Broad Institute
have to do with scale.

00:16:50.350 --> 00:16:53.900
And so I want to talk about what
I mean when I talk about scale

00:16:53.900 --> 00:16:55.970
in our GCP environment.

00:16:55.970 --> 00:16:57.930
So many of you--

00:16:57.930 --> 00:17:01.640
if you use G Suite, you might
have one G Suite domain.

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:04.730
If you use GCP, you might
have one billing account.

00:17:04.730 --> 00:17:08.480
Maybe you have dozens of
projects, potentially.

00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:10.710
But we're another order
of magnitude here.

00:17:10.710 --> 00:17:15.290
So for example, we have seven
individual G Suite domains,

00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:17.569
two of which are
production domains.

00:17:17.569 --> 00:17:21.560
And part of the reason for
that is we have a Cloud service

00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:23.819
called Fire Cloud, which
is hosted on Google.

00:17:23.819 --> 00:17:26.990
And it provides facilities for
researchers around the world

00:17:26.990 --> 00:17:29.150
to do genomic analysis.

00:17:29.150 --> 00:17:33.740
And the team that manages that
system, part of the application

00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:37.460
uses root credentials
in the G Suite domain

00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:40.300
to create groups
and create users.

00:17:40.300 --> 00:17:44.060
And so we don't want to give out
the credentials for the Broad

00:17:44.060 --> 00:17:46.801
Institute's domain
to the team that's

00:17:46.801 --> 00:17:48.050
building the software package.

00:17:48.050 --> 00:17:50.570
So they have their own
domain for Fire Cloud.

00:17:50.570 --> 00:17:53.680
And then we have a bunch of
test domains and quality and QA

00:17:53.680 --> 00:17:57.500
and other things that we use
different G Suite domains for.

00:17:57.500 --> 00:18:00.710
Across these seven domains,
primarily in our main domain--

00:18:00.710 --> 00:18:04.490
BroadInstitute.org-- we
have over 5,000 users.

00:18:04.490 --> 00:18:06.470
And this is interesting
because we only

00:18:06.470 --> 00:18:08.960
have around 1,400 employees.

00:18:08.960 --> 00:18:12.200
So it turns out that we're a
very collaborative and open

00:18:12.200 --> 00:18:13.160
institute.

00:18:13.160 --> 00:18:14.690
And there are
thousands of people

00:18:14.690 --> 00:18:17.350
from our sister institutions
around Cambridge

00:18:17.350 --> 00:18:20.940
and the Harvard teaching
hospitals and such,

00:18:20.940 --> 00:18:23.270
where they also have
identities in our environment.

00:18:23.270 --> 00:18:25.280
And they can log
in to our G Suite.

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:27.060
They can even log
into our clusters.

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:29.907
They can use the
Cloud and so forth.

00:18:29.907 --> 00:18:31.490
So it's a very, very
large environment

00:18:31.490 --> 00:18:32.510
as far as the number of users.

00:18:32.510 --> 00:18:34.593
And it's also very spread
out, because they're not

00:18:34.593 --> 00:18:37.010
all within our buildings.

00:18:37.010 --> 00:18:42.230
We have 2,500 groups, which is
about one group for every two

00:18:42.230 --> 00:18:43.490
users.

00:18:43.490 --> 00:18:45.384
And we are set up
in a way that users

00:18:45.384 --> 00:18:46.550
can create their own groups.

00:18:46.550 --> 00:18:48.290
So there's quite a few of them.

00:18:48.290 --> 00:18:51.720
And we use those to manage
Google Doc permissions

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:54.620
to manage access to
GCP projects, Google

00:18:54.620 --> 00:18:56.810
sites, any number of
things, in addition

00:18:56.810 --> 00:19:00.120
to mailing lists-- which
many of you may use them for.

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:02.840
Now, four of those
G Suite domains

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:06.630
have a corresponding
GCP org that's in use.

00:19:06.630 --> 00:19:08.690
There's one for the
Broad Institute, which

00:19:08.690 --> 00:19:11.150
our users, when they create
projects-- as you mentioned,

00:19:11.150 --> 00:19:13.400
users can create
projects and spend up

00:19:13.400 --> 00:19:16.400
resources-- they create their
projects in the Broad Institute

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:17.490
domain.

00:19:17.490 --> 00:19:19.970
But Fire Cloud actually
creates projects

00:19:19.970 --> 00:19:20.990
as part of its process.

00:19:20.990 --> 00:19:22.640
When a new user signs
up for Fire Cloud

00:19:22.640 --> 00:19:26.240
and creates workspaces and
starts doing genomic analysis,

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:28.640
there are projects that are
created in the background

00:19:28.640 --> 00:19:32.600
by service accounts that
are used by that system.

00:19:32.600 --> 00:19:35.600
And those are created in
GCP organizations that

00:19:35.600 --> 00:19:38.510
are specific to that domain.

00:19:38.510 --> 00:19:41.240
In order to organize all the
things in these organizations,

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:42.740
we have a lot of
folders, and we can

00:19:42.740 --> 00:19:45.530
apply permissions and other
controls at the folder level

00:19:45.530 --> 00:19:47.360
and at the subfolder level.

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:49.850
And this turns out to
be dozens and dozens--

00:19:49.850 --> 00:19:53.170
approaching 200
folders at this point.

00:19:53.170 --> 00:19:56.462
We have over 7,000
projects, which becomes

00:19:56.462 --> 00:19:57.670
really challenging to manage.

00:19:57.670 --> 00:20:00.003
Even if you just want to get
a list of all your projects

00:20:00.003 --> 00:20:01.960
and see the IAM
permissions on each one,

00:20:01.960 --> 00:20:05.100
that can be an
involved endeavor.

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:08.830
And maybe there's only around
800 or so in our G Suite--

00:20:08.830 --> 00:20:11.200
in our primary Broad
Institute domain.

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:14.740
And the rest of them are all
part of the Fire Cloud system.

00:20:14.740 --> 00:20:18.500
This slide here says that
I have 75,000 buckets.

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:20.654
That's actually incorrect.

00:20:20.654 --> 00:20:22.820
I learned this week that
we have a test project that

00:20:22.820 --> 00:20:26.030
has 280,000 buckets in it.

00:20:26.030 --> 00:20:28.510
And it's just most of
my systems that monitor

00:20:28.510 --> 00:20:29.894
the projects fail on that one.

00:20:29.894 --> 00:20:31.310
And I only learned
that this week.

00:20:31.310 --> 00:20:35.810
So that should say that
we have 320,000 buckets.

00:20:35.810 --> 00:20:39.830
And in those buckets, we have
over 35 petabytes, nearly 40

00:20:39.830 --> 00:20:41.154
petabytes of storage to that.

00:20:41.154 --> 00:20:43.070
And that's about half
of what we have overall,

00:20:43.070 --> 00:20:45.610
because we have about that
same amount in our legacy

00:20:45.610 --> 00:20:47.405
on-prem environment
that we still

00:20:47.405 --> 00:20:51.134
are trying to figure out how
exactly to move to the Cloud.

00:20:51.134 --> 00:20:52.800
And now we get into
the interesting part

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:53.880
with the billing.

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:56.550
We have one billing account,
which is pretty normal.

00:20:56.550 --> 00:20:58.950
And like many of you,
our billing account

00:20:58.950 --> 00:21:01.320
is a typical, monthly-invoice
billing account.

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:04.110
But we have a configuration
on our account

00:21:04.110 --> 00:21:06.630
that we're set up
like a reseller.

00:21:06.630 --> 00:21:09.600
Some of you may work with
resellers to purchase your GCP.

00:21:09.600 --> 00:21:12.330
And we're set up like that
because we resell the service

00:21:12.330 --> 00:21:14.490
to our internal organizations.

00:21:14.490 --> 00:21:18.180
And I'm going to go into this
in a lot more detail shortly.

00:21:18.180 --> 00:21:20.370
This slide shows a
bunch of the features

00:21:20.370 --> 00:21:23.107
that we use within GCP
that help with managing

00:21:23.107 --> 00:21:23.940
everything at scale.

00:21:23.940 --> 00:21:25.080
I'm actually not going
to go through this,

00:21:25.080 --> 00:21:26.621
because I'm going
to do, pretty much,

00:21:26.621 --> 00:21:28.640
a slide for each one of those.

00:21:28.640 --> 00:21:30.200
So the billing accounts--

00:21:30.200 --> 00:21:32.510
I mentioned the
monthly invoicing.

00:21:32.510 --> 00:21:34.120
I mentioned the
offline contract.

00:21:34.120 --> 00:21:36.130
I highly recommend, if
you have a business,

00:21:36.130 --> 00:21:37.720
that you're running on GCP.

00:21:37.720 --> 00:21:40.954
Look into paying
for it on an invoice

00:21:40.954 --> 00:21:42.370
instead of using
your credit card,

00:21:42.370 --> 00:21:44.710
because there are certain things
that can happen with the credit

00:21:44.710 --> 00:21:45.209
card.

00:21:45.209 --> 00:21:47.350
We've had an issue where
someone used the credit

00:21:47.350 --> 00:21:48.766
card to purchase
something online,

00:21:48.766 --> 00:21:50.944
and they mistyped the zip code.

00:21:50.944 --> 00:21:53.110
And then the credit card
company shut down the card.

00:21:53.110 --> 00:21:55.750
And so any other services that
were associated with that card

00:21:55.750 --> 00:21:56.660
would have problems.

00:21:56.660 --> 00:21:58.535
And so we certainly
don't want that to happen

00:21:58.535 --> 00:22:01.184
to our GCP environment.

00:22:01.184 --> 00:22:02.725
So the way we use
these subaccounts--

00:22:02.725 --> 00:22:04.420
let me talk about
that a little bit.

00:22:04.420 --> 00:22:07.480
Each one of these grants, and
each one of our internal cost

00:22:07.480 --> 00:22:08.649
structures--

00:22:08.649 --> 00:22:09.940
and we call them a cost object.

00:22:09.940 --> 00:22:12.580
Some people call them a
cost center or an account.

00:22:12.580 --> 00:22:15.490
Each one of those that
wants to use the Cloud

00:22:15.490 --> 00:22:17.230
gets a subaccount.

00:22:17.230 --> 00:22:18.790
And the reason we
do this is because

00:22:18.790 --> 00:22:22.460
at the end of the month-- and
we get our one bill from Google,

00:22:22.460 --> 00:22:25.270
which we pay, and
then we charge back

00:22:25.270 --> 00:22:27.970
to dozens and dozens of
internal-cost objects,

00:22:27.970 --> 00:22:31.960
the subportion of the bill
that they actually spend.

00:22:31.960 --> 00:22:33.820
So we were originally
doing this two

00:22:33.820 --> 00:22:36.010
or three years ago using
labels, which I'll talk

00:22:36.010 --> 00:22:38.980
about briefly in another slide.

00:22:38.980 --> 00:22:41.590
But the problem with using
labels for this today

00:22:41.590 --> 00:22:43.570
is that even if you
create a project

00:22:43.570 --> 00:22:46.210
and put a label on it that
says which cost center

00:22:46.210 --> 00:22:48.670
it should be billed to,
the owner of that project

00:22:48.670 --> 00:22:49.570
can change that.

00:22:49.570 --> 00:22:51.070
Or they can remove the label.

00:22:51.070 --> 00:22:54.580
And so if you rely on that, as
a way of carving up your bill,

00:22:54.580 --> 00:22:56.890
then when you get
the bill, you go,

00:22:56.890 --> 00:22:59.800
what is this huge amount that
doesn't have a cost object?

00:22:59.800 --> 00:23:01.316
And who should pay for that?

00:23:01.316 --> 00:23:02.690
And so that was
very problematic.

00:23:02.690 --> 00:23:04.810
And so two years
ago or so, we moved

00:23:04.810 --> 00:23:06.850
to this subaccounts
feature, again,

00:23:06.850 --> 00:23:09.434
which is specifically
geared towards resellers.

00:23:09.434 --> 00:23:12.100
And I think in the future, there
might be some other models that

00:23:12.100 --> 00:23:14.680
are coming that would actually
be more appropriate for us,

00:23:14.680 --> 00:23:18.740
maybe around using the
folders for example.

00:23:18.740 --> 00:23:21.370
So another thing about these
is that each one of these cost

00:23:21.370 --> 00:23:23.100
objects is owned by somebody.

00:23:23.100 --> 00:23:25.090
They are, maybe, the
principal investigator

00:23:25.090 --> 00:23:28.450
on the grant, the person who
is responsible for the funds.

00:23:28.450 --> 00:23:31.540
And they may actually have
more than one cost object

00:23:31.540 --> 00:23:32.300
that they manage.

00:23:32.300 --> 00:23:34.990
So if any of you manage
billing accounts in Google,

00:23:34.990 --> 00:23:37.090
when you manage more
than one billing account,

00:23:37.090 --> 00:23:39.670
some of the things are around
budget, some around permissions

00:23:39.670 --> 00:23:40.840
can get pretty complicated.

00:23:40.840 --> 00:23:43.640
And I'll talk about how we
work through some of that.

00:23:43.640 --> 00:23:45.550
But the nice thing about
the bill that we got

00:23:45.550 --> 00:23:47.560
is that it has one total
at the bottom, which

00:23:47.560 --> 00:23:48.880
is the amount we pay.

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:51.250
And then it has 175
subtotals, which

00:23:51.250 --> 00:23:53.620
is the amount we charge all
the internal-cost objects.

00:23:53.620 --> 00:23:56.200
And we used to have to calculate
that ourselves, so that is

00:23:56.200 --> 00:23:59.670
actually a great benefit for us.

00:23:59.670 --> 00:24:02.780
One of the other great features
that we use to manage all this

00:24:02.780 --> 00:24:06.470
is the BigQuery and
the JSON or CSV Export.

00:24:06.470 --> 00:24:08.030
We use both of these right now.

00:24:08.030 --> 00:24:09.830
The BigQuery Export
is the most valuable,

00:24:09.830 --> 00:24:12.410
and that is the newest
of these features.

00:24:12.410 --> 00:24:16.230
The CSV or JSON Export
is useful, for example--

00:24:16.230 --> 00:24:17.960
some of you may use
a third-party service

00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:20.960
to help visualize your billing
across your multiple Clouds.

00:24:20.960 --> 00:24:23.510
We've used tools
like Cloud Health,

00:24:23.510 --> 00:24:24.810
for example, in the past.

00:24:24.810 --> 00:24:27.350
And they can easily consume
your billing information

00:24:27.350 --> 00:24:29.582
from a bucket with a CSV.

00:24:29.582 --> 00:24:31.040
And they were doing
that for years,

00:24:31.040 --> 00:24:33.860
because that's how Amazon
delivers its bills for example.

00:24:33.860 --> 00:24:36.020
And so now, some
of the companies

00:24:36.020 --> 00:24:38.990
are starting to be able to use
the BigQuery Export for that

00:24:38.990 --> 00:24:40.230
too.

00:24:40.230 --> 00:24:42.230
One of the nice things
about the BigQuery Export

00:24:42.230 --> 00:24:45.590
is all the data related to your
bill is being piped directly

00:24:45.590 --> 00:24:46.320
into BigQuery.

00:24:46.320 --> 00:24:49.160
So whatever tools you might
use to deal with BigQuery,

00:24:49.160 --> 00:24:50.200
you can use to do that.

00:24:50.200 --> 00:24:51.959
So when we want to
visualize this data,

00:24:51.959 --> 00:24:53.750
try to understand what's
going on in there,

00:24:53.750 --> 00:24:56.720
we have half a dozen
different ways we can do that.

00:24:56.720 --> 00:25:00.120
And some of these I'm going to
show in my demo a little later.

00:25:00.120 --> 00:25:01.952
So for example, in
the billing console,

00:25:01.952 --> 00:25:04.160
there is a new feature that
came out this year called

00:25:04.160 --> 00:25:04.970
Billing Reports.

00:25:04.970 --> 00:25:07.760
And this allows you to see a
graph of all of the activity

00:25:07.760 --> 00:25:10.670
and to narrow it down
by SKU, and by product,

00:25:10.670 --> 00:25:11.990
and by time period.

00:25:11.990 --> 00:25:15.650
And that, for me, actually is
the best and most common way

00:25:15.650 --> 00:25:17.900
that I like to look
at the bill today.

00:25:17.900 --> 00:25:20.199
Before that existed
though, we used

00:25:20.199 --> 00:25:21.740
a lot of different
methods of looking

00:25:21.740 --> 00:25:23.040
at the data in BigQuery.

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:24.980
So for example, we
used Cloud Datalab,

00:25:24.980 --> 00:25:26.960
which is like a
IPython, Jupyter,

00:25:26.960 --> 00:25:28.820
Notebooks that Google offers.

00:25:28.820 --> 00:25:32.390
You can write reports and
display graphs and so forth,

00:25:32.390 --> 00:25:33.672
about your billing data.

00:25:33.672 --> 00:25:35.630
That was very useful for
us to determine things

00:25:35.630 --> 00:25:38.150
like how much data
we have, because you

00:25:38.150 --> 00:25:42.290
can't DU all your buckets
when you have 380,000 of them

00:25:42.290 --> 00:25:43.100
or whatever.

00:25:43.100 --> 00:25:45.140
So it's sometimes
easier to actually

00:25:45.140 --> 00:25:47.420
look at the billing data
to calculate how much data

00:25:47.420 --> 00:25:49.487
you have.

00:25:49.487 --> 00:25:51.320
DataStudio is also an
amazing tool for this.

00:25:51.320 --> 00:25:54.080
This is relatively
recent addition as well,

00:25:54.080 --> 00:25:55.430
but I absolutely love this.

00:25:55.430 --> 00:25:57.470
If you're familiar
with Google Sheets

00:25:57.470 --> 00:25:59.780
and making a chart
in Google Sheets,

00:25:59.780 --> 00:26:04.550
the user experience of doing
data analysis in DataStudio

00:26:04.550 --> 00:26:05.700
is very similar to that.

00:26:05.700 --> 00:26:09.602
It's quite easy, and it's a
free service offered by Google.

00:26:09.602 --> 00:26:11.810
And then at the Broad, we're
also a big Tableau shop.

00:26:11.810 --> 00:26:13.520
We have a lot of
investment in Tableau

00:26:13.520 --> 00:26:15.440
and a lot of
expertise in Tableau.

00:26:15.440 --> 00:26:17.210
So we've built some
very complicated

00:26:17.210 --> 00:26:20.930
and in-depth reports
to understand our GCP

00:26:20.930 --> 00:26:23.070
data using Tableau as well.

00:26:25.650 --> 00:26:28.680
Another useful feature
is custom IAM roles.

00:26:28.680 --> 00:26:30.750
We have one, and very
important, customer

00:26:30.750 --> 00:26:32.042
IAM role that we use right now.

00:26:32.042 --> 00:26:33.875
There's a few others
that we have developed,

00:26:33.875 --> 00:26:35.430
but this is probably
the most useful.

00:26:35.430 --> 00:26:38.400
We have an individual in
the procurement department

00:26:38.400 --> 00:26:42.492
who is the person that creates
new Google billing accounts.

00:26:42.492 --> 00:26:44.700
And people are not allowed
to create billing accounts

00:26:44.700 --> 00:26:45.960
except for Stewart.

00:26:45.960 --> 00:26:47.720
He has that access.

00:26:47.720 --> 00:26:50.070
And so basically the
way the process works

00:26:50.070 --> 00:26:53.880
is a user will say they would
like to begin using the Cloud.

00:26:53.880 --> 00:26:57.500
They will specify which cost
object they would like to use.

00:26:57.500 --> 00:27:00.120
And they will specify
some initial budgets,

00:27:00.120 --> 00:27:04.224
like I want to spend
$100 a month or less.

00:27:04.224 --> 00:27:05.640
And then Stuart
will go, and he'll

00:27:05.640 --> 00:27:06.977
create the billing account.

00:27:06.977 --> 00:27:09.060
And he'll give them access
to the billing account.

00:27:09.060 --> 00:27:10.601
And he'll create
that initial budget,

00:27:10.601 --> 00:27:12.450
and then he'll share
it with them, so

00:27:12.450 --> 00:27:14.700
that they can begin using it.

00:27:14.700 --> 00:27:18.960
So in order to grant him access
to all of the billing accounts,

00:27:18.960 --> 00:27:20.460
we moved all of the
billing accounts

00:27:20.460 --> 00:27:23.910
into our GCP
organization, and then we

00:27:23.910 --> 00:27:26.670
apply this permission
at the org level.

00:27:26.670 --> 00:27:29.610
And so Stuart inherits
this customer mission

00:27:29.610 --> 00:27:31.770
at the org level for
all of our resources.

00:27:31.770 --> 00:27:33.570
And so what these
three permissions do--

00:27:33.570 --> 00:27:35.653
we give him billing account
creator, which I think

00:27:35.653 --> 00:27:36.560
is pretty obvious.

00:27:36.560 --> 00:27:38.434
It allows him to create
new billing accounts,

00:27:38.434 --> 00:27:40.492
and he's the only
one that can do that.

00:27:40.492 --> 00:27:42.450
We also give him billing
account administrator,

00:27:42.450 --> 00:27:44.670
so that means he can change
the names of the billing

00:27:44.670 --> 00:27:46.290
accounts if necessary.

00:27:46.290 --> 00:27:49.350
He can add or remove
administrative users

00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:50.710
on the account.

00:27:50.710 --> 00:27:53.184
And the Project Billing
Admin permission

00:27:53.184 --> 00:27:54.600
is also very
important, because it

00:27:54.600 --> 00:27:58.770
means he can move any project
in the organization from one

00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:01.470
billing account to another,
which you can't do unless you

00:28:01.470 --> 00:28:04.720
have both access to the project
and access to the billing

00:28:04.720 --> 00:28:05.220
account.

00:28:07.920 --> 00:28:10.890
So I mentioned the org
and folders a little bit.

00:28:10.890 --> 00:28:12.840
We do have two
production organizations,

00:28:12.840 --> 00:28:15.810
one for Fire Cloud and
one for Broad Institute.

00:28:15.810 --> 00:28:18.510
And then we have a
few test organizations

00:28:18.510 --> 00:28:20.430
that we use as well.

00:28:20.430 --> 00:28:22.890
The way we've organized
our folder structure

00:28:22.890 --> 00:28:27.210
is not consistent, but we
use different mechanisms

00:28:27.210 --> 00:28:29.740
depending on the needs
in a given situation.

00:28:29.740 --> 00:28:32.040
So for example, the
first thing that we

00:28:32.040 --> 00:28:34.230
do for all of our
billing accounts

00:28:34.230 --> 00:28:37.710
is we create a corresponding
folder in the organization

00:28:37.710 --> 00:28:39.630
to map to that billing account.

00:28:39.630 --> 00:28:41.020
This does a few things for us.

00:28:41.020 --> 00:28:44.280
First of all, I'm
anticipating a future

00:28:44.280 --> 00:28:48.900
where I might be able to
actually apply the billing

00:28:48.900 --> 00:28:53.540
account to the projects
using the folder.

00:28:53.540 --> 00:28:54.530
That's not a thing yet.

00:28:54.530 --> 00:28:56.540
But in a perfect
world for us, we

00:28:56.540 --> 00:29:00.170
would like to be able to
organize projects into folders,

00:29:00.170 --> 00:29:02.750
and then have everything
in that folder inherit

00:29:02.750 --> 00:29:04.507
the billing from the parent.

00:29:04.507 --> 00:29:06.090
That makes it easier
for us to manage.

00:29:06.090 --> 00:29:07.923
So that's something
we've been anticipating.

00:29:07.923 --> 00:29:09.950
So we've organized
our organization

00:29:09.950 --> 00:29:10.910
so it's ready for that.

00:29:10.910 --> 00:29:15.180
So even though I think I started
that maybe two years ago,

00:29:15.180 --> 00:29:17.120
whenever that feature
exists, we will

00:29:17.120 --> 00:29:20.270
be ready to start using it.

00:29:20.270 --> 00:29:21.590
MATT LEONARD: It's coming soon.

00:29:21.590 --> 00:29:22.240
LUKAS KARLSSON: Oh, yeah.

00:29:22.240 --> 00:29:22.740
OK.

00:29:25.220 --> 00:29:27.800
So we have a few organizations
that are big GCP users.

00:29:27.800 --> 00:29:29.510
The IT organization,
which I'm involved,

00:29:29.510 --> 00:29:30.990
is one of those organizations.

00:29:30.990 --> 00:29:33.620
And so we have hundreds
of projects amongst us.

00:29:33.620 --> 00:29:36.200
And it's become useful to
have folders and subfolders.

00:29:36.200 --> 00:29:39.110
So for example, I have a
folder for the IT department,

00:29:39.110 --> 00:29:41.630
and then I have subfolders
for the teams within there.

00:29:41.630 --> 00:29:43.389
And the managers of
each of those teams

00:29:43.389 --> 00:29:44.930
have the right access
on the folders,

00:29:44.930 --> 00:29:46.471
so they can put
things in the folder.

00:29:46.471 --> 00:29:49.730
And they can have permission
over all their stuff.

00:29:49.730 --> 00:29:51.230
One of the other
benefits, actually,

00:29:51.230 --> 00:29:53.480
of the billing folders
that I didn't mention

00:29:53.480 --> 00:29:55.610
is that there's an
interesting thing.

00:29:55.610 --> 00:29:57.077
If you are a billing
administrator,

00:29:57.077 --> 00:29:58.910
and you own a billing
account because you're

00:29:58.910 --> 00:30:01.490
responsible for the
money, you may not really

00:30:01.490 --> 00:30:04.620
be the person who actually
does anything in GCP.

00:30:04.620 --> 00:30:06.320
So you may delegate
another person

00:30:06.320 --> 00:30:08.840
on your team to have
billing-account user

00:30:08.840 --> 00:30:09.710
permission.

00:30:09.710 --> 00:30:11.960
And that allows that person
to create as many projects

00:30:11.960 --> 00:30:14.060
as they want on your
billing account.

00:30:14.060 --> 00:30:17.270
It's analogous to giving someone
a credit card on your account

00:30:17.270 --> 00:30:19.610
and letting them do
whatever they want with it.

00:30:19.610 --> 00:30:22.280
The unfortunate
thing about that is

00:30:22.280 --> 00:30:25.070
if that person, who makes
that project on your billing

00:30:25.070 --> 00:30:27.920
account, does not give
you access to the project,

00:30:27.920 --> 00:30:31.620
you have literally no idea what
your money is being spent on.

00:30:31.620 --> 00:30:33.749
So we have people contacting
IT being like, what

00:30:33.749 --> 00:30:34.790
is all the money in here?

00:30:34.790 --> 00:30:35.915
And I'm like, I don't know.

00:30:35.915 --> 00:30:37.500
One of your people made that.

00:30:37.500 --> 00:30:39.120
Can you talk to them?

00:30:39.120 --> 00:30:40.640
And so it turns out
it's much easier

00:30:40.640 --> 00:30:44.950
if we give the billing-account
administrator, at least,

00:30:44.950 --> 00:30:47.330
viewer-level access
on the folder.

00:30:47.330 --> 00:30:51.760
And if all the projects that
are associated with that billing

00:30:51.760 --> 00:30:54.100
account are somewhere
beneath that folder,

00:30:54.100 --> 00:30:56.290
then magically the
billing-account administrator

00:30:56.290 --> 00:30:58.720
also has access
to see everything

00:30:58.720 --> 00:31:01.310
that their people are
creating, which is a huge win.

00:31:01.310 --> 00:31:03.310
And then there's some
situations where you might

00:31:03.310 --> 00:31:04.790
use folders for other purposes.

00:31:04.790 --> 00:31:09.250
For example, if I have one very
sensitive project that I only

00:31:09.250 --> 00:31:12.220
want a few people to access, and
all the rest of the projects I

00:31:12.220 --> 00:31:14.530
want a larger number
of people to access,

00:31:14.530 --> 00:31:17.050
I might, at the top
level, have those as two

00:31:17.050 --> 00:31:19.107
separate, top-level folders.

00:31:19.107 --> 00:31:21.190
And that way, I can have
a very restrictive folder

00:31:21.190 --> 00:31:23.200
that only inherits a limited
amount of permissions.

00:31:23.200 --> 00:31:25.300
And I maybe apply no
permissions at the top level

00:31:25.300 --> 00:31:26.626
in that situation.

00:31:26.626 --> 00:31:28.000
That way, your
sensitive projects

00:31:28.000 --> 00:31:29.920
can go in that sensitive,
high-level folder.

00:31:29.920 --> 00:31:31.910
And the other things
can go elsewhere.

00:31:31.910 --> 00:31:35.300
So we use that in a few places.

00:31:35.300 --> 00:31:40.380
Quotas I won't talk about too
much, for a couple of reasons.

00:31:40.380 --> 00:31:44.040
One is, if your quotas
are high enough,

00:31:44.040 --> 00:31:46.940
then they're not really
guardrails to protect you.

00:31:46.940 --> 00:31:49.520
So for example, if you
have a project where,

00:31:49.520 --> 00:31:53.280
next week, you're going to need
to run a job that uses 50,000

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:57.350
VMs, and you raise your quota
to 50,000 VMs, then now,

00:31:57.350 --> 00:31:58.890
that's what your
quota is set to.

00:31:58.890 --> 00:32:00.402
So that's not going
to prevent you

00:32:00.402 --> 00:32:01.610
from spending a lot of money.

00:32:01.610 --> 00:32:04.190
If you leave those 50,000 VMs
running, then definitely--

00:32:04.190 --> 00:32:06.510
you're certainly going to
spend a lot of money on that.

00:32:06.510 --> 00:32:09.320
And so when you run at the scale
that we do, and your quotas

00:32:09.320 --> 00:32:11.270
have to be as high
as ours are, they

00:32:11.270 --> 00:32:13.910
turn out not to
be as protective.

00:32:13.910 --> 00:32:18.740
And so again, this is
something that I would

00:32:18.740 --> 00:32:20.060
like to deal with differently.

00:32:20.060 --> 00:32:22.070
And so I've been
envisioning the idea

00:32:22.070 --> 00:32:24.770
of being able to control
this at those folder levels,

00:32:24.770 --> 00:32:25.430
as I mentioned.

00:32:25.430 --> 00:32:29.330
So maybe if I had,
at the folder level,

00:32:29.330 --> 00:32:32.750
the ability to control which
quota assigned to which things,

00:32:32.750 --> 00:32:34.520
that would be more
useful for us.

00:32:34.520 --> 00:32:37.074
And so again, we're
hoping for a future

00:32:37.074 --> 00:32:38.240
where that is a truth thing.

00:32:41.946 --> 00:32:43.237
You don't need to say anything.

00:32:43.237 --> 00:32:44.638
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:46.040 --> 00:32:49.470
So I think this
is my last slide.

00:32:49.470 --> 00:32:52.580
The budgets and
notifications are probably

00:32:52.580 --> 00:32:55.474
why I'm here to talk
with you all today.

00:32:55.474 --> 00:32:57.140
So we have dozens and
dozens of budgets.

00:32:57.140 --> 00:33:01.490
Each one of the billing accounts
has one or more budgets on it.

00:33:01.490 --> 00:33:04.340
If you've used this feature,
if you used billing accounts

00:33:04.340 --> 00:33:06.260
and budgets, then up
until earlier this year,

00:33:06.260 --> 00:33:08.690
the only way you would get
notified about these things

00:33:08.690 --> 00:33:09.890
was via email.

00:33:09.890 --> 00:33:11.540
And if you're like
me, and you happen

00:33:11.540 --> 00:33:15.200
to be a billing administrator
on a 175 billing accounts,

00:33:15.200 --> 00:33:17.360
then the number of
notifications you

00:33:17.360 --> 00:33:21.500
get from Google about a budget
that is over a threshold

00:33:21.500 --> 00:33:22.310
is a lot.

00:33:22.310 --> 00:33:23.800
It's multiple times a day.

00:33:23.800 --> 00:33:27.380
And I certainly can't react to
all of those in a sensible way.

00:33:27.380 --> 00:33:31.214
So mostly I ignore them,
which is a problem.

00:33:31.214 --> 00:33:32.880
So the addition of
Pub/Sub notifications

00:33:32.880 --> 00:33:34.077
basically changes all this.

00:33:34.077 --> 00:33:35.910
And this has been
extremely exciting for me,

00:33:35.910 --> 00:33:38.310
because I've been working
with Google on this billing

00:33:38.310 --> 00:33:40.210
stuff for a few years.

00:33:40.210 --> 00:33:44.130
And I've come up with ways
to read all the emails

00:33:44.130 --> 00:33:46.260
and try to automatically
react to the emails.

00:33:46.260 --> 00:33:48.030
But that is not
a scalable model,

00:33:48.030 --> 00:33:49.990
so this Pub/Sub thing
is really great.

00:33:49.990 --> 00:33:52.730
And I'm actually-- attempt
to show a demo of this.

00:33:52.730 --> 00:33:56.220
And we'll see if it
actually does what I want.

00:33:56.220 --> 00:33:58.230
But the purpose here
would be that you

00:33:58.230 --> 00:34:01.920
can send these notifications
to a Cloud Function,

00:34:01.920 --> 00:34:04.080
and then you can have that
Cloud Function wired up

00:34:04.080 --> 00:34:06.850
to do things that you want
in certain situations.

00:34:06.850 --> 00:34:09.330
So for example, if
there's a budget,

00:34:09.330 --> 00:34:12.239
and I know that if that budget
hits a certain threshold

00:34:12.239 --> 00:34:14.460
that there's an action I
know why I want to take,

00:34:14.460 --> 00:34:16.590
I could wire this up
with Cloud Functions.

00:34:16.590 --> 00:34:18.810
And I've attempted
to do that here.

00:34:18.810 --> 00:34:23.610
I'm not super confident, but
I'll try to as best I can.

00:34:23.610 --> 00:34:25.560
And then the final
thing that I'll mention

00:34:25.560 --> 00:34:28.530
is that we've built a bunch
of little custom tools using

00:34:28.530 --> 00:34:32.580
Google's APIs, using the IAM
APIs, and the Cloud Resource

00:34:32.580 --> 00:34:36.270
Manager, and the Billing
APIs, and others to automate

00:34:36.270 --> 00:34:37.889
a lot of the stuff that we do.

00:34:37.889 --> 00:34:40.590
So I'm going to try and
show a couple of those.

00:34:40.590 --> 00:34:42.424
There's an app that I
built like a week ago,

00:34:42.424 --> 00:34:44.131
which is the first
one I'm going to show.

00:34:44.131 --> 00:34:44.920
It's very simple.

00:34:44.920 --> 00:34:48.279
It just recreates Stuart's
job of creating and managing

00:34:48.279 --> 00:34:49.320
all the billing accounts.

00:34:52.840 --> 00:34:54.840
And then I'm going to
show this other App Engine

00:34:54.840 --> 00:34:56.100
App that I made--

00:34:56.100 --> 00:34:59.820
that its whole purpose is to
try and deal with these budget

00:34:59.820 --> 00:35:00.570
notifications.

00:35:00.570 --> 00:35:03.060
And that's where I'm a
little more skeptical about

00:35:03.060 --> 00:35:04.950
whether or not
it's going to work.

00:35:04.950 --> 00:35:06.570
Before I show the
demo, I'm going

00:35:06.570 --> 00:35:11.920
to talk about the architecture
of this billing-notifications

00:35:11.920 --> 00:35:12.700
app.

00:35:12.700 --> 00:35:14.126
I have a bunch of logos up here.

00:35:14.126 --> 00:35:16.000
I'll see if I can make
sense of this for you.

00:35:16.000 --> 00:35:18.180
So on the left-hand
side, you see, in red,

00:35:18.180 --> 00:35:19.590
the master-billing account.

00:35:19.590 --> 00:35:21.900
That is the Broad's
normal billing account.

00:35:21.900 --> 00:35:24.090
We got a bill each
month from that thing.

00:35:24.090 --> 00:35:27.580
And underneath that, you see
there's several subaccounts.

00:35:27.580 --> 00:35:30.400
So each of those sub accounts
has one or more budgets.

00:35:30.400 --> 00:35:32.800
And those budgets
are all wired up

00:35:32.800 --> 00:35:34.990
to send their information,
their notifications,

00:35:34.990 --> 00:35:37.040
to the same Pub/Sub topic.

00:35:37.040 --> 00:35:39.280
So we have one Pub/Sub
topic that is receiving

00:35:39.280 --> 00:35:41.920
a barrage of notifications.

00:35:41.920 --> 00:35:44.150
They come in maybe a
couple times an hour

00:35:44.150 --> 00:35:45.490
for every single budget.

00:35:45.490 --> 00:35:48.160
So where you normally
get notified via email,

00:35:48.160 --> 00:35:51.760
let's say once a
week or something--

00:35:51.760 --> 00:35:53.300
I don't know exactly
the frequency--

00:35:53.300 --> 00:35:55.829
these are coming in all
the time, every hour.

00:35:55.829 --> 00:35:58.120
And so whether or not you've
hit one of the thresholds,

00:35:58.120 --> 00:36:02.150
you know the state of each of
your budgets at any given time.

00:36:02.150 --> 00:36:05.050
So those all feed
into Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:36:05.050 --> 00:36:07.300
and then there's this
function at the bottom there

00:36:07.300 --> 00:36:11.140
that consumes all of the
input from that Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:36:11.140 --> 00:36:17.470
And it does some stuff, like it
stores the budget information

00:36:17.470 --> 00:36:18.830
and data-store database.

00:36:18.830 --> 00:36:21.610
So I can look at any given time
and see the status of all 175

00:36:21.610 --> 00:36:25.180
budgets without having to click
through 175 different billing

00:36:25.180 --> 00:36:26.440
accounts.

00:36:26.440 --> 00:36:29.110
It logs in in Cloud Logging,
so I know it happened.

00:36:29.110 --> 00:36:32.290
And then it uses a look-up
tool to try and figure out

00:36:32.290 --> 00:36:33.970
if it should take an action.

00:36:33.970 --> 00:36:36.670
And if it determines that
it should take an action,

00:36:36.670 --> 00:36:38.530
then it kicks off one
of these other Cloud

00:36:38.530 --> 00:36:40.270
Functions on the right there.

00:36:40.270 --> 00:36:42.640
I have one called
Disabled Billing,

00:36:42.640 --> 00:36:45.040
one called Stop
Compute Instances,

00:36:45.040 --> 00:36:46.810
and one called Notify Slack.

00:36:46.810 --> 00:36:48.580
And each of those
does, basically,

00:36:48.580 --> 00:36:49.735
what it says it does.

00:36:49.735 --> 00:36:51.610
And there's a couple of
other icons on there.

00:36:51.610 --> 00:36:53.470
I use BigQuery
because I like to look

00:36:53.470 --> 00:36:57.820
at how much spam we've had
up to this point in the year.

00:36:57.820 --> 00:36:59.860
That's a helpful,
little thing to look at.

00:36:59.860 --> 00:37:01.890
When you're looking at
the budget and the spend,

00:37:01.890 --> 00:37:02.872
that's useful.

00:37:02.872 --> 00:37:04.330
And then the Cloud
Resource Manager

00:37:04.330 --> 00:37:06.520
because one of the
things this app does

00:37:06.520 --> 00:37:09.660
is it visits each
billing account

00:37:09.660 --> 00:37:11.800
to get all of the
IAM permissions

00:37:11.800 --> 00:37:14.130
and see who the users
are associated with that.

00:37:14.130 --> 00:37:16.780
Again, that is an operation
that takes a while.

00:37:16.780 --> 00:37:19.091
And so I chache that data,
so I have easy ability

00:37:19.091 --> 00:37:21.340
to report on who is associated
with all of our billing

00:37:21.340 --> 00:37:23.620
accounts.

00:37:23.620 --> 00:37:26.080
And on the App Engine
app, users can log on,

00:37:26.080 --> 00:37:28.780
and it uses the identity
of our proxy to make sure

00:37:28.780 --> 00:37:30.277
that only Broad users log in.

00:37:30.277 --> 00:37:32.860
And the next thing is, since I
know which users are associated

00:37:32.860 --> 00:37:35.770
with which billing accounts, I
can show them only the billing

00:37:35.770 --> 00:37:38.430
accounts that relate to them.

00:37:38.430 --> 00:37:40.440
And first, let me wake
up my computer here--

00:37:45.047 --> 00:37:45.880
type in my password.

00:37:52.572 --> 00:37:55.250
Then we are going to
switch to the demo.

00:37:55.250 --> 00:37:56.731
Great.

00:37:56.731 --> 00:37:57.230
OK.

00:37:57.230 --> 00:38:03.610
So this app here is a
simple, little app that--

00:38:03.610 --> 00:38:06.110
I just built it last week, so
it only does Google right now.

00:38:06.110 --> 00:38:08.750
But it'll do other things soon.

00:38:08.750 --> 00:38:09.620
It has a list of--

00:38:09.620 --> 00:38:11.650
I guess there's 188
billing accounts now.

00:38:11.650 --> 00:38:13.660
So my slide is out
of date already.

00:38:13.660 --> 00:38:16.600
Some of them are disabled.

00:38:16.600 --> 00:38:18.730
Billing accounts
don't have a ton

00:38:18.730 --> 00:38:21.340
of things like labels
or other stuff,

00:38:21.340 --> 00:38:24.050
so we have to use the actual
name of the billing account.

00:38:24.050 --> 00:38:26.530
So when I take a billing account
that I'm not using anymore

00:38:26.530 --> 00:38:28.690
and stop using it, I changed
the name to disabled.

00:38:31.240 --> 00:38:32.607
So this is really useful.

00:38:32.607 --> 00:38:34.690
You have a list of all the
billing accounts, which

00:38:34.690 --> 00:38:36.490
cost object it's linked to.

00:38:36.490 --> 00:38:38.450
You can tell if
it's open or closed.

00:38:38.450 --> 00:38:40.990
And you can jump to the console
for that particular billing

00:38:40.990 --> 00:38:41.490
account.

00:38:41.490 --> 00:38:43.490
And I have one that I
actually made specifically

00:38:43.490 --> 00:38:48.490
for this demo, which is going
to come into play in a moment.

00:38:48.490 --> 00:38:50.310
Another thing this
app can do is it has

00:38:50.310 --> 00:38:51.600
a list of all of our invoices.

00:38:51.600 --> 00:38:53.670
I'm not showing anything useful
here, because this actually

00:38:53.670 --> 00:38:54.670
doesn't do anything yet.

00:38:54.670 --> 00:38:56.970
But the premise of this
app is that because it

00:38:56.970 --> 00:38:59.340
knows which billing accounts
are associated with which

00:38:59.340 --> 00:39:01.470
cost objects, and
because Stuart can

00:39:01.470 --> 00:39:04.500
use it to create new
billing accounts,

00:39:04.500 --> 00:39:07.660
the idea is it will
also be used to generate

00:39:07.660 --> 00:39:09.320
the internal charges.

00:39:09.320 --> 00:39:11.310
And we use SAP.

00:39:11.310 --> 00:39:14.220
So at some point, this will
calculate the bill each month

00:39:14.220 --> 00:39:16.830
and split it across
multiple things,

00:39:16.830 --> 00:39:19.830
and generate the totals.

00:39:19.830 --> 00:39:23.590
We've had a bunch of
users recently who

00:39:23.590 --> 00:39:26.050
they have a more complicated
spending situation.

00:39:26.050 --> 00:39:29.820
So instead of using one-cost
object for their project,

00:39:29.820 --> 00:39:32.700
and all the money charges
to that one cost object,

00:39:32.700 --> 00:39:35.880
they need to split the funds
across many different cost

00:39:35.880 --> 00:39:37.620
objects, which has
become complicated.

00:39:37.620 --> 00:39:39.970
So part of what this system
does is allow for that.

00:39:39.970 --> 00:39:45.030
So for example, this
group here, every month,

00:39:45.030 --> 00:39:48.540
they split whatever spend they
had across 13 different cost

00:39:48.540 --> 00:39:50.200
objects.

00:39:50.200 --> 00:39:52.980
And so this is not something
that Google supports currently.

00:39:52.980 --> 00:39:54.902
And the way we're
using these billing

00:39:54.902 --> 00:39:57.360
accounts-- so we have to create
a whole structure for this.

00:39:57.360 --> 00:39:59.109
And there's a few other
useful tools here.

00:39:59.109 --> 00:40:01.180
Like, because we know
all the cost objects

00:40:01.180 --> 00:40:03.990
and what's affiliated with
them, we can do some checking

00:40:03.990 --> 00:40:07.380
and see which billing accounts
don't have a cost object.

00:40:07.380 --> 00:40:09.360
Or apparently all these
billing accounts--

00:40:09.360 --> 00:40:11.170
the cost object has expired.

00:40:11.170 --> 00:40:13.452
That's really fun for us,
because we get the bill.

00:40:13.452 --> 00:40:14.910
We process the
bill, and then we go

00:40:14.910 --> 00:40:17.010
to charge all the internal
cost objects back.

00:40:17.010 --> 00:40:20.910
And then it turns out 10
or 12 of them are over,

00:40:20.910 --> 00:40:23.500
and we have to figure out
what the new cost object is.

00:40:23.500 --> 00:40:26.160
So this is finally a way
that Stuart, in one place,

00:40:26.160 --> 00:40:31.470
can see all of that information
and can react to it.

00:40:31.470 --> 00:40:33.390
And again, this
doesn't do much today.

00:40:33.390 --> 00:40:35.640
But when Stuart wants to
create a new billing account,

00:40:35.640 --> 00:40:36.930
he can just click there.

00:40:36.930 --> 00:40:38.880
He can create one that's
directly associated

00:40:38.880 --> 00:40:40.950
with a specific cost object.

00:40:40.950 --> 00:40:45.970
Or he can create one
that has a split.

00:40:45.970 --> 00:40:48.430
So now, the other
thing I want to show

00:40:48.430 --> 00:40:52.360
is this thing that does
all of the notifications.

00:40:52.360 --> 00:40:55.420
So as a user at the Broad,
if I were to log in to this,

00:40:55.420 --> 00:40:57.350
I would just see my
own billing account.

00:40:57.350 --> 00:41:00.310
So this demo account only
has one billing account.

00:41:00.310 --> 00:41:01.960
And that only has two budgets.

00:41:01.960 --> 00:41:05.590
But if I log in
as myself, then I

00:41:05.590 --> 00:41:07.390
have several billing
accounts and a bunch

00:41:07.390 --> 00:41:08.265
of different budgets.

00:41:08.265 --> 00:41:11.839
And I can see them all in one
place, which is really useful.

00:41:11.839 --> 00:41:13.630
Now, there's a thing
on here that I kind of

00:41:13.630 --> 00:41:15.507
created-- that most
of this is just

00:41:15.507 --> 00:41:17.590
printing out the data that
comes from those budget

00:41:17.590 --> 00:41:18.580
notifications.

00:41:18.580 --> 00:41:22.960
But this projection column
is something that I added in.

00:41:22.960 --> 00:41:25.630
Typically, if any of you
use Google billing accounts,

00:41:25.630 --> 00:41:27.130
and you get these
alerts, you have

00:41:27.130 --> 00:41:30.220
to know what the data is
to know whether you care.

00:41:30.220 --> 00:41:34.660
So if I get an alert that says,
I'm over 50% on my budget,

00:41:34.660 --> 00:41:36.510
if I look at my watch,
and I see that today

00:41:36.510 --> 00:41:39.730
is the second of the month,
well, that's a huge problem.

00:41:39.730 --> 00:41:42.830
But if today is the 24th,
then I do not care at all,

00:41:42.830 --> 00:41:44.620
and I don't even need
that notification.

00:41:44.620 --> 00:41:48.440
So what I've done is
I've tried to predict,

00:41:48.440 --> 00:41:50.740
in a lazy way, how much
the budget is going

00:41:50.740 --> 00:41:52.240
to be at the end
of the month, which

00:41:52.240 --> 00:41:53.890
is what you really care about.

00:41:53.890 --> 00:41:56.826
And so if you're at 50%
and today is the 24th,

00:41:56.826 --> 00:41:58.700
then you're really not
going to hit anything,

00:41:58.700 --> 00:42:00.140
so it's nothing to worry about.

00:42:00.140 --> 00:42:02.015
But if it's like the
second day of the month,

00:42:02.015 --> 00:42:04.460
and you're already at 50%,
then it's a big problem.

00:42:04.460 --> 00:42:06.730
And so that graph
shows how likely

00:42:06.730 --> 00:42:10.367
you are to go over
your budget at the end.

00:42:10.367 --> 00:42:12.200
Now, I'm going to skip
through some of this,

00:42:12.200 --> 00:42:13.741
because the most
important thing here

00:42:13.741 --> 00:42:15.830
is to show the notifications.

00:42:15.830 --> 00:42:17.930
So I'm going to show you
the key features that

00:42:17.930 --> 00:42:19.169
have to do with that.

00:42:19.169 --> 00:42:21.710
So in addition to a list of all
the billing accounts in here,

00:42:21.710 --> 00:42:24.770
which is very important
and apparently taking

00:42:24.770 --> 00:42:28.240
its sweet time to load,
I have a list of actions

00:42:28.240 --> 00:42:29.391
that I have invented.

00:42:29.391 --> 00:42:30.640
And so there's four right now.

00:42:30.640 --> 00:42:32.480
Only three of them work.

00:42:32.480 --> 00:42:34.480
But it's the same things
Matt mentioned earlier.

00:42:34.480 --> 00:42:38.420
I can notify Slack
instead of email.

00:42:38.420 --> 00:42:41.000
I can stop the
compute instances,

00:42:41.000 --> 00:42:44.180
which is a big hammer, but
not as big as the last one

00:42:44.180 --> 00:42:45.950
which is to turn off
billing entirely.

00:42:49.160 --> 00:42:52.310
And then I have these
thresholds that I

00:42:52.310 --> 00:42:57.710
can define in here to say, well,
when this particular budget

00:42:57.710 --> 00:43:00.410
hits this particular
limit, I want

00:43:00.410 --> 00:43:02.410
to take this specific action.

00:43:02.410 --> 00:43:03.410
And this is very simple.

00:43:03.410 --> 00:43:05.240
I only have one budget
in here, and it only

00:43:05.240 --> 00:43:07.120
has the three actions to find.

00:43:07.120 --> 00:43:11.780
But I should be able to demo
this, if things go well here.

00:43:11.780 --> 00:43:14.242
So because this
all uses Pub/Sub,

00:43:14.242 --> 00:43:16.700
I'm not actually going to spend
the next 30 minutes waiting

00:43:16.700 --> 00:43:18.680
for my account to
hit the budget,

00:43:18.680 --> 00:43:20.120
and then to notify Pub/Sub.

00:43:20.120 --> 00:43:24.660
I can just send a Pub/Sub
message to fake that.

00:43:24.660 --> 00:43:27.160
So I'm going to do that here.

00:43:27.160 --> 00:43:32.140
So let's say that this
particular budget has

00:43:32.140 --> 00:43:35.940
hit 80%, which currently
that's the only attribute I'm

00:43:35.940 --> 00:43:39.330
triggering on-- is which
threshold you have exceeded.

00:43:39.330 --> 00:43:44.530
I could also trigger off of
the cost amount, for example.

00:43:44.530 --> 00:43:47.180
But it's not doing that today.

00:43:47.180 --> 00:43:47.820
OK.

00:43:47.820 --> 00:43:51.970
So I posted this
new Pub/Sub message.

00:43:51.970 --> 00:43:52.970
Let's see what happened.

00:43:59.507 --> 00:44:00.590
Maybe it'll take a moment?

00:44:04.314 --> 00:44:05.480
MATT LEONARD: Next to Wi-Fi.

